ROLE AND OPERATING RULES
You are a senior full-stack engineer and product designer. Build AlmostMine as a real, working, interactive web app in this Google AI Studio Build project that also installs on Android and iOS as a mobile app (PWA). It must persist data, enforce permissions, feel fast and calm, and look like a professionally designed consumer startup, not a template, not a dashboard full of widgets.

Operating rules:
- Stack: React + TypeScript + Vite, Tailwind CSS, React Router, Recharts, Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore (through the Firebase integration in Build mode), and a small Node/Express server for Gemini calls.
- Gemini is called from the server only. The API key lives in project secrets, never in client code or Firestore. Keep the model name in one config constant and use the current Flash-class model Build provides.
- Do not fake anything. If a capability is unavailable here, say so, use the stated fallback, and label the UI honestly. Unfinished areas are labelled 'Coming soon' and disabled.
- No real payments, identity verification, insurance or damage protection exists. Never imply they do. Show at booking and in the footer: 'Payments are arranged directly between users. AlmostMine does not process payments, verify identity, or insure items.'
- Demo data is always labelled 'Demo data' and never counted as live activity.
- Work in the stages in section 17 and stop where told. After each stage, test in the preview, fix what breaks, and report what works, what is placeholder, and what I must do in the Firebase console. When adding anything later, preserve everything that already works.

1. PRODUCT AND THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES
Name: AlmostMine. Tagline: Own less. Access more.
Problem: people buy things they use a few times (a camera, a drill, luggage, party supplies) while the same things sit idle in other people's homes. AlmostMine lets a person list what they already own and earn from it or lend it, and lets others borrow or rent it instead of buying.
Core loop: sign in, list what you own, get requests, hand over, get it back, build trust; or need something, find it nearby, request it, use it, return it.
Users: college students, young professionals, families, apartment communities, freelancers and creators.
Launch in one campus or residential community, but communityId is a first-class field everywhere so it can scale.
Currency INR (Intl.NumberFormat en-IN). Default time zone Asia/Kolkata. Booking days are ISO date strings (YYYY-MM-DD); events use Firestore timestamps.

2. EXPERIENCE PRINCIPLES (sleek, calm, uncluttered; these are requirements)
- One primary action per screen, shown as the only filled green button. Everything else is secondary or ghost.
- Progressive disclosure: show the answer first; put methods, definitions, formulas and advanced options behind a 'How this works' drawer, expandable row or Details sheet.
- At most 4 KPI tiles visible at once, at most 1 chart per card, at most 5 items in the main navigation. No dense tables on mobile; use stacked cards.
- Generous whitespace: 24 px or more between sections, 16 px card padding, a maximum content width of 1120 px on desktop, one column on mobile.
- Plain language, short labels, no jargon on the main screens. Explanations are one sentence, with detail on request.
- Every empty state has an icon, one sentence and one action. Never show a blank page, a spinner that never ends, or a raw error.
- Interactive and responsive: optimistic updates, skeleton loaders, 150 to 200 ms subtle transitions (respect prefers-reduced-motion), toasts with Undo for destructive actions, inline editing where sensible, live previews while typing, tap and hover tooltips on charts, range toggles on charts.
- Real data only. Never display invented numbers in Live mode.

3. THE CORE JOURNEY (build this first and make it excellent)
Step A. Landing and login: a clean landing page (headline, one sentence, a Sign in with Google button, 3-step How it works, a small read-only preview of 6 featured items if any exist). All other pages require sign-in; only the landing page, How it works and Legal are public.
Step B. First-run onboarding (3 short screens, skippable): choose your community, choose a few interests (optional), then 'List your first item' or 'Browse first'.
Step C. My Items (the owner's home base, route /items). A calm grid or list of the user's items, each card showing photo, name, status chip (Active, Paused, Draft), and three tiny stats (requests, completed rentals, earned). An Add item button is always visible (floating action button on mobile).
 - Add item: a bottom sheet or 3-step flow (1 Photos and name, 2 Category, condition and value, 3 How you will share it: rent per day, free lend or swap, availability). While the user types a price show a live Fair-price hint; after step 2 show a live Idle Value preview ('Items like this are often idle; here is the estimate and why'). Preview, then Publish or Save draft. After publishing show a success state with Share link and Add another.
 - Edit anytime: tap an item to open its detail; fields are editable inline with auto-save and a visible Saved indicator; drag to reorder photos; tap calendar days to block or unblock dates.
 - Pause and resume with one tap (paused items disappear from Browse but keep their history).
 - Remove: from the item menu choose Remove. If the item has pending or approved bookings, explain and offer Pause instead (removal is blocked until those bookings are finished or cancelled). Otherwise soft-remove with an 8-second Undo toast; a Removed items section allows Restore or Delete permanently (with ConfirmDialog).
Step D. Browse (route /browse): one search box with filter chips (category, price, dates, condition) in a sheet, sort menu, result cards, and a top line 'Describe what you need' that opens Find it for me. Save this search with one tap.
Step E. Request: pick dates on a calendar that disables unavailable days, see days and recorded total, optional message, one question (would you otherwise have bought this item), the no-payment notice and the cancellation policy in a collapsed note, then Send request.
Step F. Requests (route /requests): two tabs, 'I asked' and 'Asked of me'. Each request is a card with a status chip and one next action (Approve or Decline for owners; Cancel for borrowers; Mark returned when relevant; Leave a review when done). The owner sees the borrower's Community Trust Profile in a collapsed section.
Step G. Handover and return: the booking page shows a simple timeline, handover instructions after approval, and a short category-specific checklist that both people tick.
Step H. Trust: after completion both sides can leave a short review; trust indicators update.

