8. MARKETPLACE AND BOOKING RULES (single source of truth in src/domain/rules.ts)
Categories: Cameras and creator gear; Travel and luggage; Tools and DIY; Kitchen and appliances; Party and event supplies; Sports and outdoor; Books and study; Electronics and gadgets; Furniture and decor; Musical instruments; Kids and baby; Other. Conditions: New, Like new, Good, Fair.
Listing fields: title, description, category, condition, approximate replacement value (INR), transaction type (rent, free lend, swap), price per day (rent only), min and max days, optional refundable deposit shown as information only (no deposit is collected), up to 5 images, community, general locality, house rules, handover and return instructions, blocked dates, status (draft, active, paused, removed).
- Active listing: status active, at least 1 image, valid pricing, owner not deleted.
- Booking days: inclusive dates; days = number of dates; start cannot be in the past; respect min and max.
- Allowed transitions only: pending to approved, rejected or cancelled; approved to cancelled or completed. Rejected, cancelled, completed are terminal. Enforced in code and in security rules. Every transition writes statusHistory and any needed notification.
- No overlapping approved bookings: approving writes one lock document per day at listings/{id}/lockedDays/{YYYY-MM-DD} in the same transaction that sets approved; if any lock exists the transaction aborts with a clear message. Cancelling an approved booking deletes its locks in the same transaction. Owner-blocked dates use the same collection with type blocked. Rules allow create and delete, not update, on locks.
- Completion: the owner marks Returned on or after startDate; a public ledger entry is written in the same batch.
- Cancellation policy (shown before requesting): borrowers may cancel pending requests any time; approved bookings can be cancelled with no consequence until 48 hours before start; later is recorded as a late cancellation; owner cancellations of approved bookings are recorded as owner cancellations.
- Recorded value: agreedTotal = pricePerDay x days, snapshotted at request; free and swap = 0. Booked value = approved plus completed; completed value = completed only. No money moves through the app.
- Commission: default 8 percent (setting, snapshotted per booking as commissionPct). Commission = completed value x commissionPct; owner earnings = completed value minus commission. Label as recorded, not paid.
- Completion rate = completed / (approved + completed + cancelled after approval) for bookings whose end date has passed. Approval rate = (approved + completed) / decided requests.
- Zero-booking listing: active, older than 14 days, zero requests (threshold in config). Reuse count = completed bookings per listing.
- Estimated borrower savings (an ESTIMATE): only completed bookings where the borrower answered yes to would-have-bought; purchase spend avoided = replacement value counted once per borrower and listing pair; net saving = that minus recorded fees paid for that listing. Show as an upper-bound estimate with the method.

9. FEATURES THAT SOLVE THE PROBLEM (woven into the journey, not a feature dump)
- Idle Value Score (on each My Items card and in the Add flow). Score 0 to 100 = 30 usage rarity + 20 replacement-value tier + 15 condition + 20 category demand signal + 15 comparable-price availability, weights shown. The demand component is used only with at least 5 observations; otherwise excluded with the weights re-normalised and a visible note. Shows an illustrative earnings range = median comparable price x suggested rentable days x an occupancy slider (default 15 percent), labelled Illustrative, not guaranteed earnings, plus a next step (list, lend free, swap, keep).
- Fair-Price hint (Add flow and listing page). Comparables = active listings in the same category within one condition step; with at least 5, show median and 25th to 75th percentile (linear interpolation) and how it was calculated; with fewer show 'Not enough comparable listings (n of 5 needed)'. Never invent a market price.
- Borrow vs Buy (button on every rentable listing, plus a standalone tool). Inputs: purchase price, expected resale value, rental price per day, days, transport each way, non-refundable fees, other costs, refundable deposit. Output: rent cost, net buy cost, difference, break-even days (smallest days where cumulative rent cost reaches net buy cost; if never within 365 days say Renting stays cheaper), cumulative line chart, one-sentence verdict. Refundable deposits are Temporarily committed funds, excluded unless marked forfeited. Pre-filled from the listing.
- Find it for me (top of Browse). Describe what, category, dates, budget, community. Deterministic ranking: hard filters on category and real availability against lockedDays; scoring on price fit, same community or locality, saved preferences, owner response history. Results split into Available for these dates and Not available. Each result has a short Why this one list. Never label a listing bookable without an availability check. If nothing matches, offer Post to Wishboard. Gemini may only rephrase the reasons.
- Saved searches and in-app alerts. Save from Browse. On sign-in and on opening Home, find matching listings created or reactivated since lastCheckedAt and notify the signed-in user only; update lastCheckedAt so there are no duplicates. State that alerts appear when you open the app.
- Wishboard. Post a temporary need (item, category, dates, max budget, community). Public cards show only the need, category, community, dates and the requester's public display name. Lenders respond with one of their own active listings, notifying the requester. Aggregated for demand analytics.
- Smart Exchange Checklist (booking page). Category templates in code plus owner-added items; both parties acknowledge at handover and at return with timestamps and notes. Copy: 'A checklist records what both parties agreed. It does not independently prove an item's condition.' Optional Gemini suggestions for extra items, reviewed by the owner before saving.
- Community Trust Profile (on request cards and /u/:uid). Separate indicators, never one score: completed exchanges, reviews (average shown only with at least 3), cancellation history (late and owner cancellations vs completed plus cancelled), responsiveness (median hours, only with at least 5 responses), participation (active listings, wishes fulfilled), member since. Each has a formula tooltip. Computed from the public ledger and reviews only.
- Impact Scorecard (on Me and Insights). Two separate panels: Recorded (completed exchanges, items reused, recorded earnings, fees paid) and Estimated (borrower savings). No carbon or environmental figures.
- Item Journey (on item detail, owner only): Listed, Requested, Borrowed, Returned, Reused, with reuse count.
- Optional if time remains: Ownership Audit on /items (private items not yet listed, with a keep, lend, rent, swap or donate suggestion). Do not build Rental Bundles.

10. INSIGHTS (the eight analytics use cases; calm, one card at a time)
Layout: /insights (personal, own data only) and /admin/analytics (marketplace-wide, admins only). Four tabs: What happened, Why, What next, What to do. Each tab shows its two use cases as two cards; each card shows the question as the title, one chart, a one-sentence Finding, and a Details button that opens a drawer with KPI definitions, method, data requirements, sufficiency, filters, business interpretation, recommended next step and CSV export. A top filter bar (date range, category, community) and a Live, Demo, Both toggle (default Live, with a banner that states the active mode). Save filters per user. Every card has a sufficiency badge (Sufficient, Limited, Insufficient with n and the minimum).
Architecture: deterministic TypeScript pure functions in src/analytics/ (typed inputs, typed outputs with value, n, definition id and sufficiency flag), never calling Firestore or Gemini; a separate data layer fetches and filters. Gemini (/api/ai/explain) receives only the calculated result JSON and returns a short narrative labelled AI-generated explanation; it never receives raw private records or computes numbers; a deterministic template is the fallback. Regular users never receive other users' private records (enforced in rules and queries). Save this section as docs/ANALYTICS_SPEC.md.
1 Marketplace Performance (descriptive). KPIs: active listings, requests, approved, completed, completion rate, booked value, completed value, recorded earnings, commission, category performance. Charts: weekly trend and category bars.
2 Idle Asset and Community Savings (descriptive). KPIs: completed exchanges, reuse per listing, zero-booking listings, estimated borrower savings (Estimated), recorded earnings (Recorded), idle share. Charts: reuse histogram, idle vs used by category.
3 Booking Drop-off Investigator (diagnostic). Funnel requested, responded, approved, completed with rejected and cancelled branches; segments by category, price band (quartile within category), response delay (under 2 h, 2 to 24 h, 1 to 3 days, over 3 days), lead time (under 2 days, 2 to 7, over 7) and trip length. Rates with n and 95 percent Wilson interval; flag a segment only when n is at least 10 and its interval does not overlap the overall rate; wording says associated with, never caused by.
4 Demand and Pricing Gap (diagnostic). Per category and community: active listings, open wishes, requests, approval rate, median price, supply-demand ratio = (requests + open wishes) / active listings; price versus category median with approval rates above and below. Labels Possible shortage and Possible oversupply; state the correlation caveat.
5 Seven-Day Category Demand Forecast (predictive). Daily request counts per category; baseline trailing 7-day mean; day-of-week index only with at least 6 full weeks of history; refuse to forecast under 28 days of history. Backtest on the last 14 days held out, report MAE against a naive same-day-last-week baseline, show an approximate interval from residuals. Chart: actual solid, forecast dashed with a band, labelled Forecast. Persist forecasts and back-fill actuals later.
6 Booking Cancellation Risk (predictive). Smoothed segment rate = (cancelled + k x global rate) / (terminal + k), k = 10, for lead time, trip length, price band, category, response delay and borrower prior cancellations; booking risk = average of feature rates, shown as Low, Medium or High with the top contributing features, not a calibrated probability. Needs at least 50 terminal bookings; only with at least 200 terminal live bookings offer an optional in-browser logistic regression with a time-ordered holdout and AUC and Brier score. Always show the badge 'Illustrative baseline, not a validated model' unless holdout validation was actually computed.
7 Smart Listing and Pricing Recommendations (prescriptive). A rules engine in src/analytics/rules/ with named rules, for example R-PRICE-HIGH (zero requests after 14 days and price above the category 75th percentile with at least 5 comparables), R-PHOTOS (fewer than 3 images), R-RESPONSE (median response over 24 h with elevated rejection), R-AVAIL (availability gaps on the days that attract most requests), R-REPRICE-LOW (high approval and price below category median). Each recommendation stores ruleId, evidence (metric, value, n), assumptions, suggested action, priority; no numeric uplift claims; Gemini rewrites the explanation from the evidence only; users can Dismiss or Mark done (persisted in actionPlans). Members see recommendations for their own listings only.
8 Growth Action Planner (prescriptive, admin). Ranked weekly interventions of four types: recruit supply, improve listings, follow up with slow responders, close supply-demand gaps. Priority = weighted sum of normalised demand gap size, affected requests, ease of action and data sufficiency; weights shown and adjustable with sliders that always total 100; each item shows evidence and can be dismissed or marked done.
General: predictive outputs visibly separate observed from forecast; prescriptive outputs show evidence and assumptions; when live data is insufficient, say so and offer Demo mode; never call synthetic data user activity. /admin/analytics/validation runs fixture tests of every calculation against known values and shows pass or fail.

